#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158060} :activity/id #uuid "337f60ad-4ba6-567a-96b1-400ac4b94126" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050874 :topic/id #uuid "6d5d3368-7f74-57bf-a7ad-5930abadda0c" :topic/math-academy-id 2801} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158570 :tutorial/content "We now begin our journey into mathematical proof writing. Let's start by introducing some important terminology:\n\n*An **axiom** is a mathematical statement that is assumed to be true without requiring proof.*   \n\nAxioms are the central underpinnings of **axiomatic systems.** All provable results within a system can be derived from a set of axioms using logical reasoning. \n\nFor example, most of the geometry you've studied up to this point refers to geometry within flat, two-dimensional space, otherwise known as **Euclidean Geometry**.\n\nThere are five axioms from which the *entire system* of Euclidean Geometry is based:\n\n- **Axiom 1:** *Given two distinct points, there is a line segment that joins them.*\n\n- **Axiom 2:** *Any line segment can be extended into a straight line.*\n\n- **Axiom 3:** *Given two distinct points, we can construct a circle where one point lies on the circle and whose center is at the other point.*\n\n- **Axiom 4:** *All right angles are congruent.*\n\n- **Axiom 5:** *Given a line and a point that does not lie on the line, there exists exactly one line through the point that's parallel to the line.*\n\nEverything you know about Euclidean geometry can be proved starting from just these five axioms!\n\nA **mathematical proof** is a rigorous argument that a statement is true based on a set of axioms and previously established results. \n\nMathematical proofs follow a logical sequence of steps, starting from these foundational principles and building upon them using algebraic techniques and logic to arrive at a conclusion. Any mathematical proof aims to provide a watertight, convincing argument that a statement holds true." :tutorial/id #uuid "34051bc9-c521-5aa3-ab3b-264ed6821255" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7928 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6e521f91-8e57-5f6b-bf72-91b69718b1ec" :step/math-academy-id 180556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158590 :tutorial/content "**Direct proof** is a technique for proving that an implication $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true. We start with a true statement $P,$ and then use definitions, algebraic techniques, and logic to demonstrate that $Q$ must also be true. \n\nThe outline of a typical direct proof is the following:\n\n**Statement.** *If $P,$ then $Q.$* \n\n*Proof.* \n\n**Assumption:** *Suppose $P$ is true.*\n\n*Explanation of what $P$ means using axioms, definitions, or other results.*\n\n*Algebraic techniques, logic, and further definitions or axioms are used to demonstrate, through a series of inferences, that $P$ implies $Q.$* \n\n**Conclusion:** *Therefore, $Q$ is true.*\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove parity statements using direct proof." :tutorial/id #uuid "662381ca-bb0f-5621-9bd7-d542bd91eb26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9277 :tutorial/title "Direct Proof"} :step/id #uuid "695f732d-6a1c-5afd-997d-77953fca96af" :step/math-academy-id 180557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158575 :tutorial/content "Consider the following statement:\n\n![](images/2c/2c1b99730dd8ff2296e3c8e36d3236ff45c41b8835a70d36b7f2302895d233c5.svg)\n\nNotice that this is an (implicit) universal statement of the form \n\n$$\nP \\Rightarrow Q \\quad \\equiv \\quad  \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, P(n) \\Rightarrow Q(n).\n$$\n\nLet's outline a proof schema (or template) for constructing a proof of this result:\n\n- **Step 1**: We assume $P$ is true. In other words, we let $n$ be an even integer.\n\n- **Step 2:** Next, we use the *definition* of evenness to write $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\n- **Step 3:** Then, we substitute $n=2a$ into the expression for $3n+2$ and use algebraic techniques to show that the expression can be written as $2b,$ where $b$ is an integer.\n\n- **Step 4:** Since $3n+2 = 2b$ is a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that it's even (i.e., that $Q$ is true).\n\nNote the following:\n\n- For now, we're simply constructing a \"bird's-eye view\" of how direct proof works. We haven't yet proved anything! We'll get to the details of the proof shortly.\n\n- The example concerns a statement of the form \n\n$$\n\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}.\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use the same proof template to prove parity statements of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{even}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{even}, \\qquad \\textrm{odd}\\Rightarrow\\textrm{odd}\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that we'll also need to use the definition of an odd integer. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab3af668-19b7-5577-b3b9-4de4e2e86493" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7970 :tutorial/title "Parity Statements"} :step/id #uuid "f24816de-3af5-5086-bcfc-bf09afbd6e66" :step/math-academy-id 180558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157013 :question/id #uuid "f54c74aa-3a36-52a3-ba40-d5f995fbeb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-13301"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e35ca10a-b9bf-5e11-bbe4-4ebe568cbb0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050382}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422738}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Schema"} :step/id #uuid "7f6d2624-6730-5c9e-92d8-c21732d57c2b" :step/math-academy-id 180559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158592 :tutorial/content "Recall that we wish to prove the following result:\n\n*If $n$ is even, then $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by stating that an arbitrary integer, call it $n,$ is even:\n\n**Step 1:** *Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n\nNow, we use the definition of evenness:\n\n**Step 2:** *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+2$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\n**Step 3:** *Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =3(2a)+2 \\\\  & =6a+2 \\\\  & =2{{(3a+1)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a+1$ (highlighted above), we have that $3n+2 = 2b,$ which shows that $3n+2$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$ So, we proceed as follows:\n\n*Let $b = 3a+1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =2\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{(3a+1)}} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is a multiple of $2.$*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n**Step 4:** *Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d52f994-a91b-540a-8684-02b6e55f7e34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9279 :tutorial/title "Constructing a Proof"} :step/id #uuid "9296a4f5-d6c5-5353-89da-81278f4e19ac" :step/math-academy-id 180562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158593 :tutorial/content "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that $n$ is even implies $3n+2$ is even, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n*Let $n$ be an even integer.*\n *Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$*\n\n*Substituting $n=2a$ into the expression, we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =3(2a)+2 \\\\  & =2(3a+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Let $b = 3a+1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =2(3a+1) \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is a multiple of $2.$*\n\n*Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nNotice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra in step 3. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea." :tutorial/id #uuid "77fd4e5b-6405-5112-b7d0-da7f1b17523d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9280 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Proof"} :step/id #uuid "5999aaf5-6e05-5bea-b4f7-b3a3313f3c2e" :step/math-academy-id 180563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158594 :tutorial/content "Before we look at another example, let's state some results that we'll use in our work without proof:\n\n- The sum of two integers is always an integer.\n\n- The difference between two integers is always an integer.\n\n- The product of two integers is always an integer.\n\n- Any integer is either even or odd (but never both).\n\n- The associative, commutative, and distributive laws hold for all integers.\n\nThese results aren't quite axioms because they can be proved from other, more fundamental axioms. Nonetheless, for our purposes, we can think of them as axioms: we won't prove them. Instead, we'll just assume that they're true. In math, we can use statements that were proved earlier in new proofs without re-proving them each time." :tutorial/id #uuid "0e87eece-c720-5bbc-810f-f3a213b64903" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9281 :tutorial/title "Some \"Axioms\""} :step/id #uuid "7a4b8665-d841-5105-bfa4-0b01700a7a2e" :step/math-academy-id 180564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157054 :question/id #uuid "296186e1-8e54-5697-86ad-fe52356f4b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6877"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff0294bf-aed0-529b-98bc-ea5983c7e262" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422737}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving a Linear Expression Is Even"} :step/id #uuid "ede93f8c-a8af-546e-8b99-a76504af3163" :step/math-academy-id 180565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157014 :question/id #uuid "ff0076b9-83aa-500e-8501-7eb9b4e6889d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13307"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ec1dc-7365-5ba6-bc6d-6e9a13a29cd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422741}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving a Linear Expression Is Odd"} :step/id #uuid "3216e902-09cb-572f-8554-de0f2c8fb022" :step/math-academy-id 180568 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157055 :question/id #uuid "1cb515bc-5e40-577e-9714-1e4537974b62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6882"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a1ebb1-37f3-5305-99b5-cf6ddc62e037" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422739}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Parity of a Nonlinear Expression"} :step/id #uuid "51cc127a-7abc-50c5-873d-be7b7c87da2e" :step/math-academy-id 180571 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158591 :tutorial/content "Different mathematical results have different names. Let's go through those that are commonly used.\n\n- A **theorem** is a major result for which a mathematical proof exists (e.g., the Pythagorean Theorem).\n\n- A **proposition** is a result that is less important than a theorem for which a mathematical proof exists.\n\n- A **lemma** is a small, intermediate result or proposition often used as a stepping stone in proving a larger theorem.\n\n- A **corollary** is a smaller result that follows from a theorem. This is often an important special case of a theorem.\n\n- A **conjecture** is a result that some people believe to be true, but no valid proof currently exists.\n\nOne example of a famous conjecture is the **twin prime conjecture.** A twin prime pair is an ordered pair $(p,p+2)$ such that $p$ and $p+2$ are both prime. The first few twin primes are:\n\n$$\n(3, 5), \\quad (5,7), \\quad (11,13), \\quad (17,19), \\quad ...\n$$\n\nIt's conjectured that there are infinitely many twin primes. Many mathematicians believe this to be true, but no proof of the twin prime conjecture currently exists." :tutorial/id #uuid "940fbdda-ddbd-50a5-b674-96e73d080b97" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9278 :tutorial/title "Commonly Used Terminology"} :step/id #uuid "967a4299-3ec5-55b0-8047-16a4beb2970a" :step/math-academy-id 180574}] :activity/title "Direct Proof" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157468}]}
